WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.980
it might seem intuitive that we can
predict tomorrow's weather pretty well,

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.180
but not whether it will rain on Tuesday
three weeks from now.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:14.100
But we didn't always think that way about the weather.
Our understanding of the atmosphere and

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:19.460
its patterns was forever changed by the
contributions of two exceptional pioneers:

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:23.720
MIT professors Jule Charney and
Edward Lorenz.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.020
Heralds of the modern era

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:30.240
of meteorology, Charney and Lorenz - each
in his own way - left an indelible mark on

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.460
MIT - and the world.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.800
At the height of the
Second World War, an unprecedented demand

00:00:39.809 --> 00:00:44.360
for weather forecasting expertise fueled
a nationwide effort to train a

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:50.820
generation of meteorologists. Among the
young recruits was UCLA graduate student

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:56.179
Jule Gregory Charney, who had discovered
a talent for mathematics at an early age.

00:00:56.179 --> 00:01:00.420
Anticipating the United States' entry
into World War II, and encouraged by

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:05.460
Caltech professor Theodore von Kármán, he
signed up for a course in meteorology in

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:10.280
1940, seeing an opportunity to excel.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:14.000
Meanwhile, on the other side of the country at MIT,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.880
an aviation cadet and
mathematics graduate named Edward Norton Lorenz

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:23.909
began his own training in 1942 in
the Department of Meteorology, Course 19,

00:01:23.909 --> 00:01:30.290
marking the beginning of a
lifelong connection to the Institute.

00:01:57.829 --> 00:02:03.030
When the war ended, Lorenz returned to
MIT to complete his Master's Degree and

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:09.270
PhD in meteorology. After graduating in
1948, he stayed on as a research

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:14.120
scientist working with the Victor Starr
on the general circulation of the

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:19.430
atmosphere. After publishing a landmark
paper on atmospheric energy cycles

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:23.269
called "Available Potential Energy and
the Maintenance of the General

00:02:23.269 --> 00:02:30.890
Circulation," Lorenz joined the faculty in
1955. His advisor, Victor Starr, had been

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:35.239
recruited by Henry Houghton, Head of MIT's Department of Meteorology from

00:02:35.239 --> 00:02:41.870
1942 to 1969. During his tenure, Houghton
transformed the department into a

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:46.760
cutting-edge research powerhouse. He
founded the training program attended by

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:51.290
Lorenz during the war - which grew the
department more than tenfold - and oversaw

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:56.599
the development of weather radar. Later,
in a prescient move, he nurtured MIT's

00:02:56.599 --> 00:03:00.200
growing oceanography program, with help
from the Woods Hole Oceanographic

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.780
Institution, and saw it integrated with
atmospheric research. Houghton's vision

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:09.829
and guidance laid a far-reaching
foundation for scientific advance that

00:03:09.829 --> 00:03:14.409
would shape the meteorological world for
decades to come.

00:03:17.109 --> 00:03:22.639
While Lorentz was beginning his career
in meteorological research at MIT, Jule

00:03:22.639 --> 00:03:27.829
Charney was beginning to embark on a new
adventure of his own. Following the war,

00:03:27.829 --> 00:03:34.900
Charney had returned to UCLA to complete
his doctorate in 1946. His PhD thesis,

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:39.049
"Dynamics of Long Waves in a Baroclinic
Westerly Current," was

00:03:39.049 --> 00:03:42.799
groundbreaking work, which established
the mechanism for the emergence and

00:03:42.799 --> 00:03:47.239
evolution of weather systems, and what
sets their structure size and growth

00:03:47.239 --> 00:03:51.379
rate. This was an early example of
Charney's aptitude for applying

00:03:51.379 --> 00:04:13.220
mathematics to understanding physical
processes.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:17.539
Over the next two years, working first as a
research scientist at the University of

00:04:17.539 --> 00:04:22.190
Chicago with Carl Gustav Rossby - who
founded the first meteorology program at

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:25.730
MIT in 1928 - and then as a National
Research

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:30.170
Council fellow studying in Oslo, Charney
developed his quasi-geostrophic

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:34.880
equations, which laid the foundation for
modern dynamical meteorology and

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.800
oceanography. These equations not only
allowed large-scale motions of the

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:43.850
atmosphere to be analyzed without being
overshadowed by noisy effects at smaller

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:50.770
scales; they could also be integrated
forward in time to predict the weather.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:07.940
In 1948, Charney took up an appointment
at Princeton University's Institute for

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:11.840
Advanced Study to work with
mathematician John von Neumann and

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:16.880
future MIT meteorology professor Norman
Phillips on the earliest numerical

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:21.320
weather prediction models. Their first
calculations were run on the ENIAC

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:25.670
computer and published in 1950, and in
the years that followed, Charney's

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:29.390
theoretical meteorology group
demonstrated that the quasi-geostrophic

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:33.290
equations were able to reproduce
important aspects of the general

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:38.180
circulation, and even predict the
formation of cyclones. This resounding

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:40.700
success
won widespread acceptance in the

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:45.470
meteorological community for numerical
weather prediction. The equations also

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:49.940
became the theoretical foundation of
modern dynamical meteorology and

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:58.329
oceanography, permeating every aspect of
the field.

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:03.369
Building on this momentum, Charney helped
to establish the Joint Numerical Weather

00:06:03.369 --> 00:06:08.770
Prediction Unit in 1954, a collaboration
between the U.S. Weather Bureau, Air

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:13.330
Force, and Navy.
A year later, he had a hand in starting

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:18.069
up the General Circulation Research
Section of the Weather Bureau, and while

00:06:18.069 --> 00:06:21.279
this group would eventually become
NOAA's Geophysical Fluid Dynamics

00:06:21.279 --> 00:06:26.349
Laboratory and relocate to Princeton,
Charney was already considering a move

00:06:26.349 --> 00:06:29.159
to MIT.

00:06:37.199 --> 00:06:41.919
During an interview, then Provost and
Vice President Julius Stratton asked

00:06:41.919 --> 00:06:46.779
Charney what could be done to improve
the Meteorology Department. Having always

00:06:46.779 --> 00:06:52.209
been impressed by Ed Lorenz - then still a
research scientist - Charney replied that

00:06:52.209 --> 00:06:56.169
the first thing to do would be to
promote Lorenz and his adviser Victor

00:06:56.169 --> 00:07:01.169
Starr. He also reached out to recruit his
Princeton colleague, Norman Phillips.

00:07:01.169 --> 00:07:06.219
Lorenz and Phillips were duly offered
faculty positions and Charney likewise

00:07:06.219 --> 00:07:13.649
joined MIT to direct the Atmospheric and
Ocean Dynamics Project in 1956.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:19.449
Charismatic and open-minded, Charney had
a knack for bringing people together. He

00:07:19.449 --> 00:07:23.770
quickly established an informal seminar
series on geophysical fluid dynamics, a

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:26.889
tradition that would continue for 22
years,

00:07:26.889 --> 00:07:30.400
attracting experts from around New
England in the fields of meteorology,

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:37.360
oceanography, geophysics, and applied
mathematics. During his 25 years at MIT,

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:41.949
Charney mentored countless students and
made profound lasting impacts on the

00:07:41.949 --> 00:07:46.659
field through groundbreaking research on
such diverse topics as the stability of

00:07:46.659 --> 00:07:50.860
atmospheric and oceanic flows, the
formation and evolution of weather

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:55.900
systems and hurricanes, oceanic boundary
currents, how large-scale planetary waves

00:07:55.900 --> 00:08:01.029
extend up into the stratosphere, the
formation of deserts, and how westerly

00:08:01.029 --> 00:08:07.360
winds can flip between banded and wavy
patterns. He also continued to play a

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:11.740
role in the establishment of critical
weather-related initiatives, including

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:16.060
the Global Atmospheric Research Program,
an observational effort improving

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:21.430
numerical weather prediction. One of
Charney's last contributions was a

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:27.190
pioneering study conducted for the
National Academy of Sciences. The 1979

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:32.019
Charney Report was one of the earliest
modern scientific assessments of global

00:08:32.019 --> 00:08:38.260
warming. He estimated that a sustained
doubling of atmospheric CO2 would lead

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:43.719
to a surface temperature increase of
between 1.5 and 4.5 degrees centigrade, a

00:08:43.719 --> 00:08:52.329
range that has been vigorously debated
and has stood the test of time. Though

00:08:52.329 --> 00:08:56.050
Charney and Lorenz shared a similar
background in mathematics and a mutual

00:08:56.050 --> 00:09:00.550
admiration for one another, the two never
worked together directly during their

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:05.079
time at MIT. Nevertheless, their research
interests intersected and the

00:09:05.079 --> 00:09:08.589
development of numerical weather
prediction facilitated by Charney's

00:09:08.589 --> 00:09:13.880
equations carried Lorentz's research
into atmospheric dynamics forward.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:19.009
In 1967, he published "The Nature and
Theory of the General Circulation of the

00:09:19.009 --> 00:09:25.819
Atmosphere," a treatise now considered a
classic work of scientific literature. By

00:09:25.819 --> 00:09:30.110
that time, however, a new exciting
research direction had begun to occupy

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:36.199
Lorenz's attention. In 1963, after
noticing that very slightly different

00:09:36.199 --> 00:09:41.230
initial conditions - due to roundoff
errors generated by his LGP-30 computer -

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:47.089
resulted in rapidly diverging solutions,
Lorenz published a landmark article on

00:09:47.089 --> 00:09:52.370
chaos theory, the idea that nonlinear
systems such as weather are practically

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:57.579
unpredictable,
despite their deterministic evolution.

00:10:09.399 --> 00:10:14.930
The Lorenz system, three differential
equations that relate the motions of a

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:19.819
fluid layer warmed from below and cooled
from above, is one of the fundamental

00:10:19.819 --> 00:10:25.550
systems studied in chaos theory. These
simple equations revealed startling

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:30.529
behavior, which led Lorenz to an
important insight. Though the chaotic

00:10:30.529 --> 00:10:35.810
solutions never repeat, they nevertheless
tend to look very similar, even when

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:40.910
starting from a range of initial
conditions. Run many times, the solutions

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:45.800
trace out a previously unknown
mathematical object that Lorenz termed a

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:51.110
"strange attractor." This fractal set of
solutions introduced new tools to

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:55.459
analyze nonlinear chaotic systems,
helping to answer questions like why

00:10:55.459 --> 00:11:00.939
some weather pattern geometries are
common while others seem never to occur.

00:11:00.939 --> 00:11:06.170
Continuing his study of predictability,
Lorenz demonstrated in 1969 that the

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:10.579
nonlinear interaction among motions of
many different scales could lead to loss

00:11:10.579 --> 00:11:14.569
of predictability even when the model is
perfect and the initial errors are

00:11:14.569 --> 00:11:17.350
vanishingly small.

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:26.770
This

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:31.660
theory was popularized as "the butterfly
effect," the idea that small changes can

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:36.010
have large consequences.
Lorenz's discovery not only

00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:40.240
revolutionized the understanding of
fluid turbulence and weather, but also

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:48.250
led to advances in mathematics, the
natural sciences, and engineering. Lorenz

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:53.650
was a vital member of MIT for over 60
years, serving as the Head of Meteorology

00:11:53.650 --> 00:12:00.490
from 1977 to 1981. Much beloved as an
instructor and mentor, he was repeatedly

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:05.080
honored as "Best Teacher of the Year," by
MIT graduate students, and he continued

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:08.680
to conduct research and give lectures
well after becoming a Professor Emeritus

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:16.480
in 1987. He passed away on April 16th,
2008, and three years later, MIT created

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:21.010
the Lorenz Center in his honor,
dedicated to furthering the basic

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:26.950
understanding of climate science. By
comparison, Jule Charney's tenure at

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:33.150
MIT was relatively brief. After a fierce
battle with cancer, he died on June 16th,

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:38.830
1981, just two years after publishing his
pioneering report on the connection

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:45.520
between carbon dioxide and climate. Even
so, his impact on MIT and around the

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:49.330
world stands shoulder-to-shoulder with
that of his colleague and friend, Ed Lorenz.

00:12:49.330 --> 00:12:55.690
Born 100 years ago,
both men fundamentally reshaped the

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:58.950
science of meteorology during their
lifetimes.

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:03.220
Charney saw the dawn of numerical
weather prediction and its widespread

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:08.580
influence on nearly every aspect of
atmospheric and oceanographic research.

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:13.480
Lorenz shifted our basic conception of
weather and climate from deterministic

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:18.850
phenomena to chaos, and this insight
suggested new ways to examine nonlinear

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:24.490
chaotic systems, an impact that spread
outward from meteorology to transform a

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:29.860
host of other disciplines. But perhaps
their most enduring legacy is the

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:35.930
community of people trained, taught, and
inspired by Jule Charney and Ed Lorenz.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:41.700
"What I discovered after moving to MIT
was that I what I did enjoy mostly was

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:49.110
working more individually with graduate students on thesis research and research problems, because I find

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:53.520
that my own work is greatly enhanced and
becomes much more enjoyable

00:13:53.520 --> 00:14:00.510
when I can discuss it with other people. Today, the study of
atmospheric science continues at MIT in

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:04.740
the wider context of EAPS, the Department
of Earth, Atmospheric, and Planetary

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:12.920
Sciences, formed in 1983 from the merger
of Meteorology and Earth Sciences. Now,

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:18.120
100 years later, the Program in
Atmospheres, Oceans, and Climate, within

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:23.790
EAPS, celebrates and honors the lives
of Charney and Lorenz, as their students

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:28.830
and their students' students continue to
walk in their footsteps, exploring the

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:33.890
myriad reverberations of the work they
set in motion.

